import Foundation

// MARK: - Healing Relationships Content Expansion (Entries 9–20)

extension ContentLibrary {

    static let healingRelationshipsContentExpansion: [DayContent] = [
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 5:23-24",
            scriptureText: "Therefore, if you are offering your gift at the altar and there remember that your brother or sister has something against you, leave your gift there in front of the altar. First go and be reconciled to them; then come and offer your gift.",
            devotionalTitle: "Reconciliation Before Religion",
            devotionalText: "Jesus said something shocking: your relationship with people affects your relationship with God. If someone has something against you, don't just keep worshiping — go fix it. Reconciliation is so important to God that He'd rather you pause your worship to pursue it. Broken relationships aren't just painful — they're spiritual obstacles. Take the first step today.",
            prayerText: "Lord, show me if someone has something against me. Give me the humility to go to them, even if it's uncomfortable. I don't want broken relationships to hinder my worship. Help me pursue reconciliation with the same urgency You pursue me. Give me courage for the hard conversations. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone who has something against you that you've been avoiding?",
            actionSteps: ["Reach out to one person you have unresolved tension with and take a step toward reconciliation", "Before worship this week, ask God if there's a relationship that needs attention first"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ephesians 4:26-27",
            scriptureText: "In your anger do not sin: Do not let the sun go down while you are still angry, and do not give the devil a foothold.",
            devotionalTitle: "Deal With It Before Sunset",
            devotionalText: "Paul doesn't say don't get angry — anger is human. He says don't let it fester. Unresolved anger becomes a foothold for the enemy — resentment, bitterness, grudges. The 'sunset rule' is practical: deal with conflict today. Don't carry it into tomorrow. The longer anger sits, the harder it becomes to remove. Address it now, before it hardens into something worse.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I bring my anger to You before the sun goes down. Help me deal with it honestly and quickly. Don't let it become bitterness or resentment. Remove any foothold the enemy has gained through my unresolved conflict. Give me the courage to address what needs addressing today. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there anger you've been carrying past sunset that needs to be addressed today?",
            actionSteps: ["Before bed tonight, resolve or release any anger you're carrying", "If you can't resolve a conflict today, write out your anger honestly to God as a prayer"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 18:21-22",
            scriptureText: "Then Peter came to Jesus and asked, 'Lord, how many times shall I forgive my brother or sister who sins against me? Up to seven times?' Jesus answered, 'I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times.'",
            devotionalTitle: "Forgiveness Without Limits",
            devotionalText: "Peter thought he was being generous with seven. Jesus blew past his number to make a point: forgiveness isn't something you ration. It's not a finite resource that runs out. Jesus forgives you endlessly, and He asks you to extend the same to others. This doesn't mean ignoring harm or skipping boundaries. It means refusing to let unforgiveness poison your soul.",
            prayerText: "Lord, expand my capacity to forgive. Where I've been keeping score, help me stop counting. You forgive me endlessly — help me extend that same grace to others. I don't forgive because they deserve it; I forgive because You've forgiven me. Release me from the prison of unforgiveness. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone you've been rationing forgiveness toward? What would unlimited forgiveness look like?",
            actionSteps: ["Pray for someone who has hurt you repeatedly and choose to forgive again", "Write 'I forgive you' for each offense you're holding onto, then tear up the paper"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Romans 12:18",
            scriptureText: "If it is possible, as far as it depends on you, live at peace with everyone.",
            devotionalTitle: "As Far As It Depends on You",
            devotionalText: "Paul is realistic: peace isn't always possible. Some people won't reconcile. But the standard is clear: do your part. As far as it depends on you, pursue peace. You can't control the other person's response, but you can control your effort. Have you done everything you can? That's what God asks. The outcome is in His hands.",
            prayerText: "Lord, I want to live at peace with everyone. Show me what depends on me and give me the courage to do it. Where I've contributed to conflict, help me own it. Where I can't control the outcome, help me release it. I'll do my part and trust You with the rest. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Have you done everything that depends on you to be at peace with the people in your life?",
            actionSteps: ["Identify one relationship where you could take a step toward peace and take it today", "Release one relationship outcome you can't control and give it to God"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Proverbs 15:1",
            scriptureText: "A gentle answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger.",
            devotionalTitle: "The Power of a Gentle Response",
            devotionalText: "In heated moments, you have two options: escalate or de-escalate. A gentle answer doesn't mean you're weak or that you agree. It means you're wise enough to know that harsh words make everything worse. Gentleness is strength under control. It's choosing to respond instead of react. One gentle sentence can defuse what an hour of arguing cannot.",
            prayerText: "Lord, put a guard over my mouth. When I want to respond harshly, give me gentle words. Help me de-escalate instead of escalate. I know harsh words cause damage I can't undo. Fill me with Your gentleness — not weakness, but strength under control. Let my words bring peace, not pain. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In your most recent conflict, did your words turn away wrath or stir up anger?",
            actionSteps: ["Practice pausing for 5 seconds before responding in your next tense conversation", "Choose one relationship and commit to only using gentle words for the next 24 hours"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Colossians 3:12-13",
            scriptureText: "Therefore, as God's chosen people, holy and dearly loved, clothe yourselves with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness and patience. Bear with each other and forgive one another if any of you has a grievance against someone. Forgive as the Lord forgave you.",
            devotionalTitle: "Clothe Yourself for Relationship",
            devotionalText: "Paul describes five garments for healthy relationships: compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience. You put them on intentionally, like getting dressed in the morning. They don't come naturally — they're chosen. And when you're dressed in these, bearing with others and forgiving grievances becomes possible. Get dressed before you engage.",
            prayerText: "Lord, today I clothe myself with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience. These don't come naturally, so I need Your help to put them on. Help me bear with difficult people and forgive as You've forgiven me. Dress me in Your character before I face my relationships today. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Which of the five garments — compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, patience — do you most need to put on today?",
            actionSteps: ["Choose one 'garment' to focus on today and intentionally wear it in every interaction", "Before a difficult conversation, pause and 'get dressed' in these five qualities"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "James 1:19-20",
            scriptureText: "My dear brothers and sisters, take note of this: Everyone should be quick to listen, slow to speak and slow to become angry, because human anger does not produce the righteousness that God desires.",
            devotionalTitle: "Quick to Listen, Slow to Speak",
            devotionalText: "Most relationship damage comes from the opposite: being quick to speak, slow to listen, and fast to anger. James reverses the order. Listen first. Really listen — not just waiting for your turn to talk. Speak slowly and carefully. And let anger be your last response, not your first. This simple rhythm could transform every relationship you have.",
            prayerText: "Lord, make me quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to become angry. Reverse my natural tendencies. Help me truly hear what others are saying before I respond. Guard my mouth from hasty words and my heart from quick anger. Let my relationships be marked by patience and understanding. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "In your closest relationship, are you more often quick to listen or quick to speak?",
            actionSteps: ["In your next conversation, focus entirely on listening — don't think about your response until they finish", "Count to 10 before responding the next time you feel anger rising"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Corinthians 13:4-7",
            scriptureText: "Love is patient, love is kind. It does not envy, it does not boast, it is not proud. It does not dishonor others, it is not self-seeking, it is not easily angered, it keeps no record of wrongs. Love always protects, always trusts, always hopes, always perseveres.",
            devotionalTitle: "Love's Definition",
            devotionalText: "This passage isn't just for weddings — it's the blueprint for every relationship. Replace the word 'love' with your name: are you patient? Kind? Keeping no record of wrongs? These aren't feelings; they're choices. Healing broken relationships starts with choosing to love according to God's definition, not yours. It's hard. But it's the only love that lasts.",
            prayerText: "Lord, redefine love in my heart according to Your standard. Make me patient when I want to rush. Make me kind when I want to retaliate. Help me keep no record of wrongs. I choose to love as You love — not as a feeling, but as a decision. Heal my relationships through this kind of love. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Replace 'love' with your name in this passage. Where do you fall short?",
            actionSteps: ["Pick one quality from 1 Corinthians 13 and practice it intentionally in one relationship today", "Destroy any 'record of wrongs' you've been keeping — mental or written"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Galatians 6:1",
            scriptureText: "Brothers and sisters, if someone is caught in a sin, you who live by the Spirit should restore that person gently. But watch yourselves, or you also may be tempted.",
            devotionalTitle: "Restore Gently",
            devotionalText: "When someone wrongs you or falls into sin, God's first instinct isn't punishment — it's restoration. And He calls you to the same: restore gently. Not with a lecture, not with shame, not with 'I told you so.' Gently. And Paul adds a warning: watch yourself. The person who restores harshly is often one step away from their own fall. Approach others' failures with the humility of someone who knows they could be next.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me a restorative heart. When people fall, help me reach down to lift them up, not stand over them in judgment. Make me gentle in the way I address others' failures, remembering my own. Protect me from self-righteousness and fill me with compassion. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "When someone wrongs you, is your first instinct to restore or to punish?",
            actionSteps: ["Think of someone who has fallen and pray for their gentle restoration", "Examine your own heart for any self-righteousness and confess it to God"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Ephesians 4:15",
            scriptureText: "Instead, speaking the truth in love, we will grow to become in every respect the mature body of him who is the head, that is, Christ.",
            devotionalTitle: "Truth Wrapped in Love",
            devotionalText: "Truth without love is brutality. Love without truth is sentimentality. Healthy relationships need both. Speaking the truth in love means caring enough to be honest and loving enough to be kind about it. It's not either/or — it's both/and. The result isn't just a better conversation; it's mutual growth toward Christlikeness.",
            prayerText: "Lord, help me speak truth in love. Give me courage to say what needs to be said and wisdom to say it with kindness. I don't want to be brutal or sentimental — I want to be Christlike. Let my honest words bring growth, not destruction. Help me and the people around me grow toward maturity. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Do you tend to err on the side of truth without love, or love without truth?",
            actionSteps: ["Practice speaking one difficult truth to someone today, wrapped in genuine love and care", "Ask someone you trust: 'Is there a truth you've been hesitant to share with me?'"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "Matthew 7:3-5",
            scriptureText: "Why do you look at the speck of sawdust in your brother's eye and pay no attention to the plank in your own eye? How can you say to your brother, 'Let me take the speck out of your eye,' when all the time there is a plank in your own eye?",
            devotionalTitle: "Start With Your Own Plank",
            devotionalText: "It's always easier to see what's wrong with someone else. Jesus uses humor to make a serious point: your own issues are bigger than you think, and your judgment of others is often hypocritical. Before you try to fix someone else's relationship failures, deal with your own. Self-examination isn't weakness; it's the prerequisite for helping anyone else.",
            prayerText: "Lord, show me my planks. Before I point out what's wrong with others, help me deal with what's wrong in me. Give me the humility to examine myself first. Remove my tendency to judge others while ignoring my own failures. Start the healing in my relationships by starting the healing in me. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "What 'plank' in your own eye have you been ignoring while focusing on someone else's speck?",
            actionSteps: ["Before your next critical thought about someone, ask: 'What's my own plank here?'", "Confess one of your own relational failures to God and ask for His help to change"]
        ),
        DayContent(
            scriptureReference: "1 Peter 3:8-9",
            scriptureText: "Finally, all of you, be like-minded, be sympathetic, love one another, be compassionate and humble. Do not repay evil with evil or insult with insult. On the contrary, repay with blessing, because to this you were called so that you may inherit a blessing.",
            devotionalTitle: "Repay With Blessing",
            devotionalText: "Peter's instruction goes against every human instinct: when someone hurts you, bless them. When insulted, don't insult back. This isn't about being a doormat; it's about being so secure in God's love that you can absorb pain without returning it. Repaying evil with blessing breaks the cycle of hurt. It's the most powerful thing you can do in a broken relationship.",
            prayerText: "Lord, give me the supernatural ability to repay evil with blessing. When I'm hurt, help me respond with grace instead of revenge. Break the cycle of pain in my relationships. I choose blessing over bitterness. Help me be sympathetic, compassionate, and humble even when others are not. In Jesus' name, amen.",
            reflectionPrompt: "Is there someone who has hurt you that you could bless instead of resent?",
            actionSteps: ["Do something kind for someone who has wronged you — a prayer, a gift, or a kind word", "When you feel the urge to retaliate, pause and ask 'How can I bless instead?'"]
        ),
    ]
}
